  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA stalled frontal boundary is just east of the Memphis metro as\nof 10 AM. On the 'cool' side of the boundary, dewpoints are in\nthe low to mid 60s with a few post frontal showers on going. The\nfront is expected to continue its descent this afternoon over an\nairmass with upper 60s to low 70 dewpoints. Moisture and weak\nlift from the boundary is likely to initiate isolated coverage\nsouth of I-40 this afternoon. Diurnal heating will allow for very\nsteep surface lapse rates and ~1500 DCAPE. This would warrant a\ndamaging wind/downburst threat in the strongest storms, however,\noverall shear profiles are disorganized and effective shear is\nless than 20 kts. Confidence in severe storms this afternoon and\nevening is low, but a strong to severe storm cannot be ruled out.\n\nBy tomorrow, the front should be well clear of the region and a\nfew lingering residual showers should end by sunset. Northern\nportions of the region will feel slightly cooler Tuesday, but\nWednesday will be a taste of cooler weather for everyone.\nTemperatures will generally be in the 80s through the rest of the\nworkweek under northwest flow aloft. An upper level ridge will\nbuild in to our west on Friday afternoon sending 500 mb temps\nabove the 90th percentile just west of the Mid-South.\nTemperatures closest to the ridge may briefly rise above 90 this\nweekend in our western zones, but humidity will be tolerable. As\nof now, minimal rain chances are expected within the northwest\nflow regime, but we will keep an eye on rain chances. In the long\nterm, the LREF does hint at a cool down with 500 mb temperatures\nin the 25th or lower percentiles to round out September.\n\nDNM",
